{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>What gain is it that we kill our brother.</b> For this is not vengeance, for all the dead are forgotten; and so David said: 'Do not slay them, lest my people forget.'" "<b>What gain.</b> Whoever blesses Judah is effectively blaspheming, because he saved Joseph with the expression <b>'what gain'</b> — which implies: if there were gain, we would have killed him. And of this it is said: 'And he who blesses the one who cuts gain, blasphemes HaShem.'" "<b>And we will cover his blood.</b> Rashi: and we will conceal his death — like 'and it will be covered (ונעלם),' which we render (in Aramaic) 'and it will be covered (והא מכסה).' That is, we will need to cover and conceal his death, and we will not be able to boast of it, because of our father's grief. And the practice in the world is: when a person takes vengeance on his enemy, the vengeance counts for nothing if he does not boast of it."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {1 "The phrase ובוצע ברך נאץ is rendered as Chizkuni cites it, linking 'one who cuts (בוצע)' to Judah's argument about gain (בצע); the syntax is elliptical as in the Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:47.920447104Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}